# Example 12: Multi-species codon preference
# The same protein (M-A-L-E-V-I-F-G-S) is encoded with each species' optimal codons
# Demonstrates HelixLang's variable translation table / species-adaptation background
#
# Species optimal-codon differences (synonymous-codon third-base wobble):
#   E. coli  : GCG(Ala) CTG(Leu) GAA(Glu) GTG(Val) ATT(Ile) TTT(Phe) GGC(Gly) AGC(Ser)
#   Yeast    : GCT(Ala) TTA(Leu) GAA(Glu) GTT(Val) ATT(Ile) TTT(Phe) GGT(Gly) TCT(Ser)
#   Human    : GCC(Ala) CTG(Leu) GAG(Glu) GTG(Val) ATC(Ile) TTC(Phe) GGC(Gly) AGC(Ser)
#
# Each #gene ORF is scored with the relative synonymous codon usage (RSCU)
# table of every #sim species (bio_data.cai, Sharp & Li 1987).  A gene
# codon-optimized for its own species scores the highest CAI there and
# drops against the other translation tables.

#promoter name=p_ecoli strength=-0.3
#promoter name=p_yeast strength=-0.3
#promoter name=p_human strength=-0.3

# E. coli optimal-codon version (protein: M-A-L-E-V-I-F-G-S)
#gene name=gene_ecoli promoter=p_ecoli
ATG GCG CTG GAA GTG ATT TTT GGC AGC TAA
#end

# Yeast optimal-codon version (same protein, wobble positions biased to A/T)
#gene name=gene_yeast promoter=p_yeast
ATG GCT TTA GAA GTT ATT TTT GGT TCT TAA
#end

# Human optimal-codon version (same protein, wobble positions biased to C/G)
#gene name=gene_human promoter=p_human
ATG GCC CTG GAG GTG ATC TTC GGC AGC TAA
#end

#regulate p_ecoli -> gene_ecoli strength=+0.9
#regulate p_yeast -> gene_yeast strength=+0.9
#regulate p_human -> gene_human strength=+0.9

#config backend=fba ticks=1
#sim kind=codon_usage species=ecoli,yeast,human
